---
import BaseLayout from "../../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro";
import PromptPage from "../../../components/PromptPage.astro";

const masterPrompt = `Act as an experienced productivity coach and operations planner.

TASK:
Create a practical weekly work plan based on the priorities, deadlines, meetings, workload and responsibilities below.

ROLE / JOB:
[Your role or profession.]

WEEK:
[Example: Monday 7 September to Friday 11 September.]

MAIN RESPONSIBILITIES:
[List your regular responsibilities.]

TOP PRIORITIES THIS WEEK:
[List the most important outcomes you need to achieve.]

TASKS:
[List all known tasks.]

DEADLINES:
[List deadlines and due dates.]

MEETINGS / FIXED COMMITMENTS:
[List meetings, appointments, calls, inspections or fixed activities.]

ONGOING PROJECTS:
[List projects currently in progress.]

FOLLOW-UPS:
[List people, customers, suppliers, colleagues or departments you need to follow up with.]

ADMIN TASKS:
[Reports, approvals, emails, documentation, filing, system updates, etc.]

PROBLEMS / RISKS:
[List anything that could delay or disrupt the week.]

AVAILABLE WORKING HOURS:
[Example: Sunday–Thursday, 8:00 AM–5:00 PM.]

PREFERRED WORK STYLE:
[Example: focused blocks, flexible schedule, morning deep work, etc.]

SPECIAL REQUIREMENTS:
[Any additional instructions.]

WEEKLY PLANNING REQUIREMENTS:

1. DEFINE THE WEEKLY OUTCOMES
Identify the most important results that should be achieved by the end of the week.

Separate:

Must achieve
Should achieve
Could achieve

Do not treat every task as equally important.

2. PRIORITIZE TASKS
Classify tasks using:

P1 — Critical / urgent
P2 — High priority
P3 — Normal priority
P4 — Low priority

Consider:

- deadline
- business impact
- dependency
- customer impact
- operational impact
- financial impact
- effort
- risk of delay

3. IDENTIFY FIXED COMMITMENTS
List all non-movable activities first.

Examples:

- meetings
- interviews
- audits
- site visits
- deliveries
- reporting deadlines
- customer calls
- inspections

Build the flexible work around these commitments.

4. IDENTIFY DEPENDENCIES
For each important task identify:

Task:
Depends on:
Blocking issue:
Person / department involved:
Action required:

Do not schedule dependent tasks as if they can start immediately when they are blocked.

5. ESTIMATE EFFORT
Estimate each task as:

Quick — under 30 minutes
Short — 30–60 minutes
Medium — 1–2 hours
Deep Work — 2+ hours

If effort is uncertain, mark it:

"Estimate required."

6. IDENTIFY DEEP-WORK TASKS
Highlight tasks requiring:

- concentration
- analysis
- writing
- planning
- complex decisions
- report preparation
- problem solving

Schedule these during the user's strongest focus periods where possible.

7. GROUP SIMILAR TASKS
Batch similar activities such as:

- emails
- approvals
- calls
- reporting
- data review
- documentation
- follow-ups

Avoid unnecessary context switching.

8. DAILY WORKLOAD BALANCE
Do not overload one day while leaving another almost empty.

Balance:

- high-focus work
- meetings
- admin
- follow-ups
- routine duties
- urgent operational tasks

9. BUFFER TIME
Leave realistic buffer for:

- unexpected issues
- urgent requests
- delays
- meetings running over
- operational problems

Do not schedule 100% of available working time.

10. MONDAY / FIRST WORKDAY
Use the first workday for:

- weekly review
- priority confirmation
- urgent follow-ups
- planning
- starting critical tasks

Avoid filling the entire first day with low-value admin.

11. MIDWEEK REVIEW
Include a short midweek checkpoint.

Review:

- completed priorities
- delayed tasks
- new urgent work
- blocked items
- workload changes
- deadline risk

12. FINAL WORKDAY
Reserve time for:

- completion
- outstanding follow-ups
- reporting
- documentation
- next-week preparation
- review of incomplete tasks

13. DEADLINE PROTECTION
For important deadlines:

- schedule work before the actual due date
- include review time
- identify dependencies
- add contingency where appropriate

Do not schedule critical work to finish at the exact deadline unless unavoidable.

14. FOLLOW-UP MANAGEMENT
Create a follow-up list.

For each:

Person / department:
Topic:
Required action:
When to follow up:
Next step if no response:

15. EMAIL / COMMUNICATION BLOCKS
Where useful, group email and communication into defined periods instead of interrupting focused work continuously.

16. MEETING PREPARATION
For important meetings include:

- preparation time
- required documents
- decisions needed
- questions
- follow-up actions

17. ADMIN CONTROL
Schedule repetitive admin work efficiently.

Examples:

- timesheets
- reports
- approvals
- document filing
- system updates
- expense claims
- routine emails

18. HIGH-RISK ITEMS
Identify tasks at risk because of:

- dependency
- unclear ownership
- missing information
- resource shortage
- tight deadline
- customer dependency
- approval delay

Give a mitigation action for each.

19. QUICK WINS
Identify useful tasks that can be completed quickly without distracting from major priorities.

20. DELEGATION OPPORTUNITIES
Identify tasks that may be delegated.

For each:

Task:
Suggested responsible role:
Reason:
Required instruction:
Follow-up required:

Do not assume delegation authority if the user has not stated it.

21. TASKS TO DEFER
Identify lower-priority tasks that can reasonably be moved to a later week.

Explain why.

22. OVERLOAD CHECK
If the total workload appears unrealistic, clearly state:

"The current workload may exceed available time."

Then recommend:

- defer
- delegate
- reduce scope
- renegotiate deadline
- remove low-value work
- request support

Do not pretend every task can fit.

23. DAILY PLAN
For each workday provide:

Main objective:
P1 tasks:
P2 tasks:
Meetings:
Follow-ups:
Admin:
Deep-work block:
Buffer:
End-of-day check:

24. TIME-BLOCKING
Where working hours are provided, create realistic time blocks.

Example:

08:00–08:30 — Review & urgent messages
08:30–10:30 — Priority project
10:30–11:00 — Follow-ups
11:00–12:00 — Meeting
13:00–14:30 — Analysis / report
14:30–15:00 — Email block
15:00–16:00 — Operational tasks
16:00–16:30 — Buffer
16:30–17:00 — Daily review

Adjust to the actual schedule.

25. FLEXIBLE VS FIXED
Clearly distinguish:

FIXED
Cannot easily move.

FLEXIBLE
Can move if urgent work appears.

26. PRIORITY CONFLICTS
If two high-priority tasks conflict, compare:

- deadline
- consequence of delay
- stakeholder importance
- dependency
- time required

Recommend which should come first.

27. WAITING TASKS
Create a separate list for tasks waiting on:

- approval
- information
- customer
- supplier
- colleague
- system access
- management decision

Include a follow-up date or trigger.

28. WORK-IN-PROGRESS CONTROL
Avoid starting too many major tasks simultaneously.

Recommend finishing or advancing a small number of high-value tasks before opening additional work where possible.

29. INTERRUPTION STRATEGY
For roles with frequent interruptions, recommend:

- protected focus periods
- urgent escalation rules
- grouped responses
- realistic buffer
- clear priority reset after interruption

30. ENERGY / FOCUS MANAGEMENT
Where useful:

Use high-focus periods for:
- analysis
- writing
- decisions
- problem solving

Use lower-energy periods for:
- emails
- admin
- filing
- simple follow-ups

31. DAILY TOP THREE
For each day identify the three most important outcomes.

If these three are completed, the day should still be considered productive.

32. WEEKLY SCORECARD
Create a simple scorecard:

Weekly priority:
Target:
Status:
Blocker:
Next action:

Use:

Not Started
In Progress
Completed
Blocked
Deferred

33. PROGRESS TRACKING
Recommend a simple daily tracking format:

Completed:
In progress:
Blocked:
New tasks:
Carry forward:

34. END-OF-WEEK REVIEW
Include:

What was completed?
What was delayed?
Why?
What needs to move forward?
What should be stopped?
What should be delegated?
What should become next week's priority?

35. NEXT-WEEK PREPARATION
Identify:

Carry-forward tasks
Upcoming deadlines
Follow-ups
Known meetings
Risks
Preparation required

36. MANAGEMENT UPDATE
If relevant, create a concise optional weekly update:

Completed:
In progress:
Risks / blockers:
Support required:
Next priorities:

37. OUTPUT STYLE
Keep the plan:

- practical
- realistic
- easy to scan
- prioritized
- flexible
- not over-scheduled

Do not create a motivational or generic productivity plan.

OUTPUT FORMAT:

1. Weekly Objectives
2. Priority Summary
3. Fixed Commitments
4. Workload & Dependency Review
5. Weekly Risk / Blocker List
6. Daily Schedule
7. Daily Top Three
8. Follow-Up List
9. Waiting-On List
10. Delegation Opportunities
11. Tasks to Defer
12. Weekly Scorecard
13. Midweek Review
14. End-of-Week Review
15. Next-Week Preparation
16. Optional Management Update

IMPORTANT:
- Do not assume every task can fit into the week.
- Protect important deadlines with preparation time.
- Do not overfill the calendar.
- Keep buffer for unexpected work.
- Prioritize outcomes rather than activity.
- Distinguish urgent from important.
- Identify blocked work instead of repeatedly scheduling it.
- Group similar tasks where practical.
- Keep deep work separate from routine admin.
- Recommend delegation or deferral when workload exceeds capacity.
- Do not invent meetings, deadlines or responsibilities.`;

const steps = [
  {
    title: "Start with outcomes",
    description:
      "Define what actually needs to be achieved by the end of the week before filling the calendar with individual tasks.",
  },
  {
    title: "Protect the important work",
    description:
      "Place deadlines, meetings and high-focus tasks first, then fit routine admin and lower-priority work around them.",
  },
  {
    title: "Leave room for reality",
    description:
      "Keep buffer time for interruptions, unexpected work and tasks that take longer than planned instead of scheduling every available minute.",
  },
  {
    title: "Review and adjust",
    description:
      "Use a short midweek checkpoint to move priorities, follow up blocked work and prevent important deadlines from slipping.",
  },
];

const example = {
  input: [
    "Role: Warehouse supervisor.",
    "Main priorities: Complete monthly KPI report, close two receiving discrepancies and prepare for Friday stock count.",
    "Meetings: Tuesday operations meeting at 10:00 AM and Thursday customer review at 2:00 PM.",
    "Regular work: Daily floor supervision, emails, approvals and shift handover.",
    "Risk: Inventory team is waiting for documents from receiving.",
  ],

  output: [
    "Weekly priority 1: Complete stock-count preparation before Friday rather than leaving setup to the final day.",
    "Weekly priority 2: Resolve or formally escalate both receiving discrepancies after checking the missing documentation dependency.",
    "Weekly priority 3: Complete the KPI report with review time before submission.",
    "Monday should focus on confirming stock-count scope, requesting missing receiving documents and starting KPI analysis.",
    "Tuesday planning should protect time around the fixed operations meeting rather than scheduling a major deep-work task through it.",
    "Thursday should include preparation time before the customer review plus a final stock-count readiness check.",
    "Friday should keep buffer around the physical count instead of filling the day with normal administrative work.",
  ],
};

const tips = [
  {
    title: "Don't schedule 100% of the week",
    description:
      "A realistic plan needs buffer for urgent requests, interruptions, delays and work that takes longer than expected.",
  },
  {
    title: "Protect work before deadlines",
    description:
      "Important tasks should normally be completed early enough to allow review, corrections and unexpected delays.",
  },
  {
    title: "Blocked tasks need follow-up, not rescheduling",
    description:
      "When work depends on someone else, track the dependency and next follow-up instead of repeatedly placing the same blocked task on the calendar.",
  },
];

---

<BaseLayout
  title="Weekly Work Planner Prompt | KRIYANO"
  description="Create a realistic weekly work plan with priorities, deadlines, meetings, deep-work blocks, follow-ups, buffers and workload management."
>
  <PromptPage
    category="WORK & PRODUCTIVITY"
    title="Weekly Work"
    accentTitle="Planner."
    description="Turn priorities, deadlines, meetings and routine responsibilities into a realistic weekly plan that protects important work without overloading your schedule."
    meta={[
      "Free prompt",
      "Weekly planning",
      "Productivity",
    ]}
    bestFor={[
      "Professionals",
      "Managers",
      "Supervisors",
      "Office teams",
      "Operations roles",
      "Busy workweeks",
    ]}
    masterPrompt={masterPrompt}
    introTitle="Plan outcomes, not just a longer to-do list."
    introDescription="Organize fixed commitments, important deadlines, deep work, routine admin and follow-ups into a practical week with enough flexibility for real-world interruptions."
    steps={steps}
    exampleTitle="Turn a busy week into a workable plan."
    example={example}
    tipsTitle="Make the weekly plan realistic."
    tips={tips}
    sidebarTitle="Too many tasks competing for attention?"
    sidebarDescription="Use KRIYANO's Task Prioritization Prompt to rank competing work before building your weekly schedule."
    warningTitle="A good weekly plan should still be flexible"
    warningText="Unexpected work, changing priorities and operational problems can make a fixed schedule unrealistic. Review the plan during the week and adjust priorities rather than trying to follow an outdated schedule."
  />
</BaseLayout>